In this lesson, we'll look at how to test and refine conjectures so you can trust the patterns you spot in graphs and tables. Here's the key idea — a conjecture is just a claim, and a claim is only as strong as the evidence behind it. So instead of checking one or two inputs and calling it done, a good test picks several different inputs, covers edge cases like very large values, zero, and negatives, and compares what your conjecture predicts to what actually comes out. The refinement loop is simple. First, state your conjecture clearly. Then test it with multiple inputs. If every single one matches, great — you keep it. But if something disagrees, that's actually useful. You adjust the claim, maybe narrowing it from "this is always true" to "this only holds when x is greater than zero," and then test again. Common digital tools make this fast. You can build a table of values in a spreadsheet, use sliders to watch how the graph responds, or plot a few functions at once and look for where they cross. Right now, in Mathematical Methods, a conjecture backed by tested evidence is the goal — formal proof comes later. Now let's see it in action.